  "content": {
    "id": "p10-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Show that if $ax^3+bx^2+cx+d=0$, where $a\\ne0$, has a triple root, then it must occur at $x=-\\dfrac{b}{3a}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "At a triple root $r$, $P''(r)=0$. Thus $6ar+2b=0$, giving $r=-\\dfrac{b}{3a}$.",
      "worked": "Let $P(x)=ax^3+bx^2+cx+d$ and let $r$ be its triple root.\n$$\\begin{aligned}P'(x)&=3ax^2+2bx+c\\\\P''(x)&=6ax+2b.\\end{aligned}$$At a triple root, $P''(r)=0$, so\n$$\\begin{aligned}6ar+2b&=0\\\\6ar&=-2b\\\\r&=-\\frac{b}{3a}.\\end{aligned}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 25
  },
